// Filmstock's List System: THE MATRIX

// The matrix is a list of lists.

// When you allocate a list with lnew(), it gets added at
// the end of the list of lists.

// You access the individual indexes with the
// "pointers" (indexes in THE MATRIX).

// You cannot free lists.
// You can nuke all lists with lreset(), which resets everything

var a = lnew()  // lnew() returns the pointer.

printNum(a)     // This will print the pointer.
                // The pointers start at 0 and increment by 1.
nl()


// You can use lists as strings
a.setTo("Hello World\n")
// This will print the string
a.print()
nl()

// This will print the ASCII values
a.printNum()
nl()

// And yes, list functions are syntax sugar for:
// lFUNCTIONNAME
lprint(a)
lprint(0)
lprint(10 - 10)

// Let's do something goofy and sort the letters
a.sort(0)       // The zero says that the list will be sorted in ascending order.
                // Use 1 for descending order.

a.print()       // Now it's giberrish

a.empty()       // Let's empty the contents list

print("\n")     // The new line also got sorted, so let's print an another one

a.printNum()    // As we see, an empty List
